WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.940
All right, today we are going to be speaking
with senior adviser for the Joe Biden for

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:12.539
president campaign, Cristobal Alex, who has
worked on you've been working on campaigns

00:00:12.539 --> 00:00:13.539
for a long time.

00:00:13.539 --> 00:00:14.629
I mean, you're not new to this.

00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:15.629
That's right.

00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:16.990
I'm I'm a bit of an old timer.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:18.070
You can tell from my grades.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:22.870
I've had a couple of these campaigns behind
me, Maria, though, they called me and the

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.020
campaign.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.500
So let's I want to talk let's start maybe
by talking about the Latino vote.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.500
I've been speaking on my show, but also with
my audience over on my Spanish language show

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.610
on the Australia Network about the Latino
vote and the growing importance demographically

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.120
of the Latino vote in the United States.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:46.380
I was slightly disappointed, but not altogether
surprised that a higher share of the Latino

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.329
vote did not go to Joe Biden.

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:51.540
Now, I know that there's a number of ways
that we could spin this.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.100
We could look at specific states.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:59.400
We could look at sort of the range of of the
vote of the Latino vote that typically goes

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:00.890
to the Republican candidate.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.880
And certainly what Donald Trump got is within
that range.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.390
But is there any disappointment on your side
that it was not an even more overwhelming

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:11.720
victory for Joe Biden among that group?

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:17.461
You know, I'll say that we're very proud of
the work of the campaign and all of the work

00:01:17.461 --> 00:01:24.390
that preceded the campaign in critical battleground
states around the country, decades of organizing

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:32.630
work from young people, from immigration rights
organizations to Puerto Rican organizations

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:38.090
to others that have led to this election,
which I think is a an important moment if

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:42.090
you look at the trajectory of building Latino
political power.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:44.450
I think we're approaching a high watermark.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:50.080
And yes, the Latino community is, as you know
better than anybody is, is not a monolith.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:56.070
And so we do want to look more specifically
at data from states or even within states.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:02:02.220
And when we set out in the general election
with our Latino program, we set a goal to

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.650
match the high watermark of the 2012 Obama
Biden reelection campaign that hit seventy

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.289
one percent of the Latino vote.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.150
We are just about there as far as the numbers
are calculated and tabulated.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:21.540
We're at about seventy, seventy one percent
range right now in some of the more crucial

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.680
battleground states that's nationally.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.370
If you look at some of them, if you drill
down a little bit, you look at a place like

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:33.120
Wisconsin, for example, where we had a hundred
thousand plus Latino vote turnout in those

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.150
places.

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.060
Latinos really exceeded that, hitting almost
80 percent.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:43.319
If you look at Maricopa County, we flipped
Arizona and Pima County, you're looking at

00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:45.200
over 80 percent, some places.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.870
Eighty five percent of the of the vote.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:52.520
It's also the reason why we kept we flip to
Arizona.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:53.950
That's why we kept Nevada.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:03:01.349
That's why we elected a United States senator,
Latino United States senator in New Mexico.

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:07.410
And if you look at Pennsylvania, a huge surge
of Latino voters, a lot of what a lot of Puerto

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:08.410
Ricans there.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:13.460
And again, because of the hard work that preceded
the campaign and proudly, I will say, because

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:17.870
of the campaign's significant investments,
over one hundred million dollars to reach

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:24.000
Latino voters, we exceeded almost that 80
percent mark in Pennsylvania, again, proving

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.040
to be the margin of difference.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.560
We're very excited about that.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:27.720
Yeah, OK, good.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:28.880
So those are some good examples.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.730
I mean, let's let's now talk about one that's
an area of concern to me.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:39.599
We've known for a long time that while the
American Latino voter community is not a monolith,

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:46.780
one of the it is a very left community in
total, with the notable exception of Cuban-Americans.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:52.360
And we know about this sort of anti Castro
reflexive thing and fears of so-called socialism,

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:57.780
which will often influence particularly Florida
Cuban-Americans to vote at higher levels for

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.550
Republicans than than we see in other states.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:07.349
It would be huge, would it not, to be able
to bring over more of that Cuban-American

00:04:07.349 --> 00:04:09.360
community in Florida?

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:16.500
Because without Florida, the map for Republican
Republicans nationally gets systemically very,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:17.500
very difficult.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:23.319
And I'm wondering what you think it will take
to bring over more Cuban-American voters?

00:04:23.319 --> 00:04:25.229
That's a great question.

00:04:25.229 --> 00:04:32.789
I'll start by saying Miami Dade and South
Florida, a true battleground.

00:04:32.789 --> 00:04:38.259
And we we fought very hard and competed for
every single voter there.

00:04:38.259 --> 00:04:42.669
And we did win by a good margin, the Latino
vote in Florida.

00:04:42.669 --> 00:04:49.029
We've got some work to do with with Cubans
and also Venezuelans and others.

00:04:49.029 --> 00:04:55.539
And if you look a little deeper, I think President
elect did very well with younger voters, young

00:04:55.539 --> 00:04:56.559
Latino voters, especially.

00:04:56.559 --> 00:04:59.879
Those were very high vote share for us.

00:04:59.879 --> 00:05:01.639
But we've got some work to do.

00:05:01.639 --> 00:05:03.960
It was some of the older voters, especially
the Cuban voters.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:11.169
And I'll say it's hard to compare this election
to 2016 when Secretary Clinton got a lot of

00:05:11.169 --> 00:05:13.639
support from the Cuban community.

00:05:13.639 --> 00:05:19.389
Part of the reason for that was, if you remember,
the presidential primary in 2016.

00:05:19.389 --> 00:05:24.629
On the Republican side, Donald Trump spent
a lot of time attacking Marco Rubio, for example,

00:05:24.629 --> 00:05:29.509
among other things, that that brought down
his percentage among Cuban voters.

00:05:29.509 --> 00:05:34.009
But as you pointed out, that usually a higher
percent goes Republican there.

00:05:34.009 --> 00:05:35.439
So we are going to do some work there.

00:05:35.439 --> 00:05:42.580
And I think part of it we'll see trending
in our direction very soon is once President

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:48.219
elect Joe Biden and takes office along with
his vice president, Kamala Harris, we're going

00:05:48.219 --> 00:05:54.319
to do a lot of work to make sure that we can
expand democracy in Cuba.

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:59.639
Then we strengthen relationships throughout
Latin and Central America and make sure that

00:05:59.639 --> 00:06:05.240
we're doing the things that we need to do
that will eventually again, this is going

00:06:05.240 --> 00:06:09.020
to be a good policy, but eventually will yield
a better percentages.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.020
And some of the.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.710
These communities, when we look at the politics
of it, yeah, you mentioned that this year

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:19.449
Donald Trump was not attacking Marco Rubio
in the way that he was in 2016.

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:23.169
One of the other differences I noticed, and
I wonder if you if you think this is relevant

00:06:23.169 --> 00:06:26.469
electorally, is that merely by chance?

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:30.550
Not because Donald Trump's views have become
more enlightened, but merely by chance.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:35.089
There was much less discussion about Mexican
rapists and criminals in the last six months.

00:06:35.089 --> 00:06:39.650
There was much less discussion about the wall,
although at rallies, Trump would say the wall

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:40.771
is being built in Mexico.

00:06:40.771 --> 00:06:45.930
Mexico's paying for it, obviously, both wise
by by sheer distraction, it seems, from what's

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.610
been going on.

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.039
Trump did less of that.

00:06:49.039 --> 00:06:53.409
And I'm wondering if that also ended up helping
him a little bit in the end.

00:06:53.409 --> 00:06:54.749
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:06:54.749 --> 00:07:04.240
I wonder that myself and I did expect a lot
more of the typical vitriol demagoguing Latino

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.619
communities, especially immigrants.

00:07:06.619 --> 00:07:12.919
And it was there no doubt, just not on the
front page or the first thing out of his mouth

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:15.719
that we would see in twenty eighteen or twenty
sixteen.

00:07:15.719 --> 00:07:16.719
Right.

00:07:16.719 --> 00:07:24.449
Part of the reason for that is undoubtedly
the fact that our country is facing a pandemic

00:07:24.449 --> 00:07:30.129
and the economy is is is in bad shape again
because of Donald Trump's failed leadership.

00:07:30.129 --> 00:07:31.750
And that was top of mind for voters.

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:36.559
And he tried very hard, no doubt, to to try
and make the election anything about himself

00:07:36.559 --> 00:07:37.749
or his failed leadership.

00:07:37.749 --> 00:07:44.409
But at the end, it becomes a referendum on
his handling of covid is complete and utter

00:07:44.409 --> 00:07:45.879
failure there.

00:07:45.879 --> 00:07:49.099
And so it was hard for him to to turn that
script.

00:07:49.099 --> 00:07:57.939
Now, I will say, even though he spoke perhaps
less about immigrants and didn't attack Latinos

00:07:57.939 --> 00:08:03.159
and immigrants as much as he did previously,
his administration wasted no time trying to

00:08:03.159 --> 00:08:05.649
to do harm to the communities.

00:08:05.649 --> 00:08:11.189
And one of the first things that you will
see president will act when he becomes president,

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:17.149
Joe Biden do is undo much of the harm that
Donald Trump and his administration has done.

00:08:17.149 --> 00:08:18.149
Yes.

00:08:18.149 --> 00:08:19.149
I want to talk about that next.

00:08:19.149 --> 00:08:20.149
So let's get into that.

00:08:20.149 --> 00:08:24.069
We've started to see this list of possible
day one executive orders.

00:08:24.069 --> 00:08:29.550
We've heard about getting back in the Paris
climate, trans military ban.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:33.370
Maybe something can be done in terms of the
trade relationship with China via executive

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:34.370
order.

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:38.719
And there's this question mark around DACA
and and dreamers.

00:08:38.719 --> 00:08:40.789
What is the day one plan?

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:44.660
What can be done via executive order with
that program?

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.089
Quite a bit, actually.

00:08:46.089 --> 00:08:51.930
In our new chief of staff, White House Chief
of Staff Ron Klain last week talked about

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:55.720
some of those day one priorities and one of
those.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:09:02.589
And it's very important to Joe Biden and Kamala
Harris is protecting dreamers.

00:09:02.589 --> 00:09:06.310
So there will be executive action on day one.

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:13.350
And there will also be an immigration bill
that includes, obviously, dreamers and their

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:19.660
families and and a pathway to citizenship,
a roadmap to citizenship for the 11 million

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:21.019
undocumented in this country.

00:09:21.019 --> 00:09:23.709
That's that's going to happen right away.

00:09:23.709 --> 00:09:27.300
Now, of course, we're going to have to do
a lot of work to get the immigration bill

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:28.300
passed.

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:32.800
But through executive action, there's going
to be a very swift undertaking to protect,

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:34.220
to protect dreamers.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:40.600
Yeah, I think that if Democrats don't take
the Senate tax reform and the health care

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:43.790
expansion that Joe Biden wants to do, I think
they don't happen.

00:09:43.790 --> 00:09:50.660
I do think that because some of these immigration
issues are actually relevant to some Republican

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:55.250
senators, I think that the chance of doing
something on immigration is slightly higher

00:09:55.250 --> 00:09:57.589
if even without having the Senate.

00:09:57.589 --> 00:10:00.699
But a lot is riding on those races in Georgia,
isn't it?

00:10:00.699 --> 00:10:02.250
A lot's riding on those races.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:05.839
And what I will say is we're hopeful.

00:10:05.839 --> 00:10:09.569
And again, you saw Georgia turning blue.

00:10:09.569 --> 00:10:15.660
You're seeing a record enthusiasm, a record
vote there, a very diverse electorate.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.620
All of those things bode well.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:24.889
But I think it's important to remember Joe
Biden has a very distinguished public service

00:10:24.889 --> 00:10:27.110
career in the Senate.

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:32.270
He knows how to work across the aisle to get
big things done for America, whether that

00:10:32.270 --> 00:10:39.940
was beating the NRA not once but twice, pushing
for the very first climate change legislation

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:45.740
when he was in the Senate that was advocating
and getting past the Violence Against Women

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:46.740
Act.

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:47.740
I mean, the list goes on and on.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:49.720
He does very well in these situations.

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:58.020
I mean, health care, again, the ACA, another
prime example of him working hard and being

00:10:58.020 --> 00:11:02.100
able to to get things done with Democrats
and with Republicans.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:06.750
And so you can expect four major pieces of
legislation like that for Joe Biden to do

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.509
what he does best, which is to try to work
with.

00:11:09.509 --> 00:11:14.459
Others to unify not just our country, but
but to unify our government, to tackle these

00:11:14.459 --> 00:11:15.459
major challenges.

00:11:15.459 --> 00:11:22.080
Yeah, I don't doubt for a second Joe Biden's
ability, willingness and interest to do that.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:26.970
I just think it's naive to think these Republicans
are going to participate in anything that

00:11:26.970 --> 00:11:31.960
doesn't obviously and completely benefit what
what they've laid out as their priorities.

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:36.339
I think the Republican Party has changed dramatically,
but I remain cautiously optimistic.

00:11:36.339 --> 00:11:41.000
I think the concern among some in the progressive
left is what would Joe Biden have to compromise

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.910
in order to get some of these Republicans
on board?

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.980
You know, here's the thing, I.

00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:55.000
As a progressive myself, I think what what
we need to remember here is the country is

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.120
on this precipice right now.

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:04.209
As Joe Biden talked about this during the
campaign, in our darkest times, our nation

00:12:04.209 --> 00:12:09.100
has been able to come together and achieve
the greatest good.

00:12:09.100 --> 00:12:12.689
If you look at some of the moments in history,
whether it's coming out of the Great Depression,

00:12:12.689 --> 00:12:20.100
whether it's coming out of World War Two,
we're able to come together and do amazing

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:21.699
things.

00:12:21.699 --> 00:12:28.180
And I know it sounds naive, but I am optimistic
that we are at one of these moments now where

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:33.149
we're going to have to do we're going to have
to, number one, get this pandemic, the virus

00:12:33.149 --> 00:12:34.149
under control.

00:12:34.149 --> 00:12:40.009
But then there's an opportunity, and partly
because we have to to rebuild this economy.

00:12:40.009 --> 00:12:45.450
And there are a lot of major components that
will go into that, including major investments.

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:53.670
Again, we're going to work with Republicans
and conservatives and progressives and independents

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:55.380
and all Americans to get this done.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.300
But we need to be able to rebuild the economy.

00:12:57.300 --> 00:13:01.339
We need to make sure that we get climate change
under control.

00:13:01.339 --> 00:13:06.339
And there's ways where we can build millions
of jobs in this country that are better green

00:13:06.339 --> 00:13:07.339
jobs.

00:13:07.339 --> 00:13:08.339
And so that's part of it.

00:13:08.339 --> 00:13:09.339
And that's one of the things we need to do.

00:13:09.339 --> 00:13:16.129
And the other is with all of the the racial
justice issues that are now getting a lot

00:13:16.129 --> 00:13:19.629
of the attention that they that they deserve,
there's going to be a way to address some

00:13:19.629 --> 00:13:22.620
of these systemic racial issues in our country.

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:26.810
Again, even though we're dealing with this
pandemic and having to rebuild this economy,

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.329
there are opportunities within that to address
some of these things.

00:13:29.329 --> 00:13:35.499
And we're I mean, no, no, no disagreement
from me in terms of the stakes or the priorities,

00:13:35.499 --> 00:13:41.170
skepticism, significant skepticism about the
current Republican Party as as a participant

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:42.170
in that.

00:13:42.170 --> 00:13:45.250
But we will we will very soon start to see
how it plays out.

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:50.899
We've been speaking with Cristobal Alex, who
is a senior adviser for the Joe Biden for

00:13:50.899 --> 00:13:51.899
President campaign.

00:13:51.899 --> 00:13:53.110
I really appreciate your time today.

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:54.459
Oh, it was an honor.

00:13:54.459 --> 00:13:55.459
Thanks for having me.

00:13:55.459 --> 00:13:56.459
The David Pakman Show at David Pakman Dotcom.

00:13:56.459 --> 00:13:56.467
The David Pakman Show at David Pakman Dotcom.

